Q: Why does @Transactional not work on an inner / same-class method call?

Most common reason: Spring’s **proxy-based** transaction management + **self-invocation**.

────────────────────────────────────────
1️⃣ The Problem (Self Invocation)

Spring applies @Transactional through a proxy around the bean.
A call from inside the same class uses `this.method()` → **bypasses the proxy** → `@Transactional` on that inner method is ignored.

Example (inner @Transactional does NOT start):

@Service
public class OrderService {

    public void createOrder() {
        saveOrder();   // this.saveOrder() — no proxy
    }

    @Transactional
    public void saveOrder() {
        // expected transaction may NOT start
    }
}

Flow:

Client → Proxy → createOrder()
                    ↓
               this.saveOrder()   ← direct call, proxy skipped
                    ↓
            @Transactional ignored

────────────────────────────────────────
Important nuance

If the **outer** method is already `@Transactional`, DB work in the inner method still runs in that outer transaction
(same thread / persistence context) — even on self-call.

Self-invocation is the problem when you need the **inner** method’s own annotation to apply, e.g.:
- only inner method has `@Transactional`
- inner has `Propagation.REQUIRES_NEW` (must suspend outer and start a new TX)
- inner has different rollback/isolation settings

────────────────────────────────────────
2️⃣ Solution 1 (Recommended) — another Spring bean

@Service
public class OrderService {

    @Autowired
    private OrderPersistenceService orderPersistenceService;

    public void createOrder() {
        orderPersistenceService.saveOrder(); // goes through that bean’s proxy
    }
}

@Service
public class OrderPersistenceService {

    @Transactional
    public void saveOrder() {
        // transaction works
    }
}

Flow: Client → … → Proxy(OrderPersistenceService) → saveOrder()

────────────────────────────────────────
3️⃣ Solution 2 — call through proxy (self inject)

@Autowired
@Lazy  // often needed to avoid circular dependency on self
private OrderService self;

public void createOrder() {
    self.saveOrder(); // proxy → @Transactional applies
}

────────────────────────────────────────
4️⃣ Solution 3 — AspectJ mode (less common)

Switch transaction weaving to AspectJ so self-invocation is also advised.
Usually overkill; prefer separate bean.

────────────────────────────────────────
5️⃣ Other reasons @Transactional “doesn’t work”

- Method not public (Spring proxy AOP typically needs public)
- Class not a Spring bean (missing @Service / @Component)
- Exception swallowed (try-catch without rethrow / setRollbackOnly)
- Checked exception without rollbackFor
- Wrong propagation
- Calling from outside Spring context (new OrderService())

────────────────────────────────────────
6️⃣ Interview one-liner

@Transactional is applied via a Spring AOP proxy, so same-class (self) calls bypass the proxy
and the inner method’s transactional advice is not applied.
Fix by moving it to another bean, calling through an injected proxy, or using AspectJ.
